# Liquid glass controls

Buttons, icon buttons, chips, steppers, segmented controls, and other tappable chrome use iOS liquid glass when the device can render it. Fall back to the existing mist/solid styles otherwise. Put the effect in shared controls — screens should not wrap ad-hoc `Pressable`s in `GlassView`.

## When to use glass

Use `expo-glass-effect` only after all of these are true:

- `isLiquidGlassAvailable()`
- `isGlassEffectAPIAvailable()`
- Reduce Transparency is off (`AccessibilityInfo.isReduceTransparencyEnabled()`)

Keep that check in one hook (for example `useLiquidGlass`). Catch native-module errors and treat them as unavailable.

`isInteractive` belongs on actual controls, not cards or backgrounds. `colorScheme` is `'light'`. Tint with tokens (`Colors.SeafoamMuted`, `Colors.Seafoam` for primary).

## Implementation

On glass, drop the solid fill and hairline; keep size, radius, and label color. Do not set `overflow: 'hidden'` on `GlassView` or an ancestor. Do not animate `opacity` on `GlassView`.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — opaque chrome that never uses glass
<SIconBtn onPress={onPress}>
  <Ionicons name='watch' />
</SIconBtn>

// ✅ GOOD — interactive glass when available, mist button otherwise
if (liquid) {
  return (
    <GlassView isInteractive glassEffectStyle='regular' colorScheme='light' tintColor={Colors.SeafoamMuted}>
      <SIconBtn $glass onPress={onPress}>{icon}</SIconBtn>
    </GlassView>
  )
}
return <SIconBtn onPress={onPress}>{icon}</SIconBtn>
```
